Ladybird lays out the columns of a table whose direction is rtl
from the right now: It sizes the columns in the table’s own column
order, and places the cells, the column boxes and the collapsed
borders from the right. Blink gets the same result by laying the
table out in logical coordinates under the table’s writing direction,
in TableLayoutAlgorithm::GenerateFragment(), and resolves collapsed
borders by that direction in TableBorders. WebKit mirrors each cell’s
inline position against the column positions in RenderTableSection::
logicalRectForWritingModeAndDirection() when the table’s writing
mode is inline-flipped. Gecko places cells in logical coordinates of
the table’s writing mode too, in nsTableRowFrame::ReflowChildren().
Chrome 158 and Firefox 151 give exactly Ladybird’s cell and column
positions now, including percentage priority and the originating
column of a spanning cell, and WebKit 26.5 differs only by a pixel
in collapsed-border tables.
